1.5.11.m:
[With] "Great may the aid of the three be", he attends [upon the fire] with a triplet addressed to Prajāpati.
Belonging to Prajāpati indeed are these offspring;
them indeed he secures;
them he makes edible.
Then he approaches Prajāpati himself;
in Prajāpati alone among the deities does he firmly establish himself.
[With] "The soma-presser, the sounding one", he attends [upon the fire] with [a verse] addressed to Bṛhaspati;
he firmly establishes himself in the Brahman;
then he secures Brahmanical splendor.
Whoever contends with one established in the Brahman falls before him.
Both kinds of deities indeed expect [oblations] from the Agnihotrin: those to whom he offers, and those to whom he does not.
[With] "Of Mitra who supports the peoples", he attends [upon the fire] with [a verse] addressed to Mitra;
from both sides indeed, from here and from yonder, he makes Mitra with this [verse].
[With] "Never are you barren", he attends [upon the fire] with two bṛhatī verses addressed to Indra.
Belonging to Indra indeed are cattle;
them indeed he secures;
them he makes edible.
[With] "Around you the invulnerable chariot":
yonder sun indeed is the invulnerable chariot;
this one indeed traverses both these worlds;
he encompasses all indeed with this [verse].
[With] "You are the crusher;
may I crush down him who hates us and whom we hate":
he should press down with his heel if he contends with an inferior.
[With] "You are the overcomer;
may I overcome him who hates us and whom we hate":
he should press down with the right side of the foot if he contends with an equal.
[With] "You are the prevailing one;
may I prevail over him who hates us and whom we hate":
he should press down with the forepart of the foot if he contends with a superior.
All of them indeed he overcomes;
all of them he surpasses;
all of them he steps beyond.
[With] "May Pūṣan, path-protector, protect me":
[this is] this [earth] indeed.
[With] "May Pūṣan, cattle-protector, protect me":
[this is] the intermediate space indeed.
[With] "May Pūṣan, overlord, protect me":
[this is] yonder [sky] indeed.
Unto these worlds indeed he has approached;
for these worlds he protects himself,
for non-injury.
[With] "The eastern quarter, Agni the deity":
he deposits body-protections in the quarters.
Then he with whom he contends or who acts wrongfully toward him, encountering these very deities, perishes first.
Seven indeed are the greatnesses in man;
they indeed are to be praised by him;
they indeed are the seven ṛṣis;
the life-breaths indeed are the seven ṛṣis;
the life-breaths indeed he praises with this.
He praises his own life-breaths;
he strips off the life-breaths of his rival.
No one assailing him lays him low, who knows thus.
[With] "May law protect me from unrighteousness;
may dispersion protect me from dispersion;
life and forward-life, sight and distinct sight, onward and downward, and Uruka":
this [breath] indeed is Uruka;
he distributes;
what this one distributes, that he allots back to this one.
"O Agni, lord of the house, the sacrificer kindles the fire":
this indeed is the sacrificer's own, namely the fire;
this is Agni's, namely the sacrificer;
this is made like a proper dwelling.
"To light, to the thread, to you": having sat down between the two fires, he should say [this].
Whatever blessing he prays for before, whatever after, that he places in himself.

1.6.4.m:
The gods indeed could not divide the fire.
When they carried it to the east, it was entirely in front;
when they carried it to the west, it was entirely behind.
They carried it away by means of a horse drawing first.
That is the first-drawing horse's nature of drawing first.
For the division of the fire indeed a horse is given at the fire-establishment.
Undivided indeed is his fire, unestablished, who does not give a horse at the fire-establishment.
Now he who gives a horse at the fire-establishment,
[it is] for division;
having divided it, he establishes it.
With the stoma leading, indeed, the gods conquered the Asuras.
This horse, truly, is the stoma.
When they lead a horse in front,
[it is] for conquest;
having conquered, he establishes it.
Prajāpati's eye indeed swelled;
what swelling he had, that became the horse.
When they lead a horse in front,
he bestows the eye upon the sacrificer.
It is not to be released facing away.
If he were to release it facing away, the eye would leave the sacrificer;
he would be blind.
Having held it facing back, [the fire] is to be established;
he has held back the eye for the sacrificer.
It is not to be established in [the horse's] footprint;
he would make it belong to the homestead;
Rudra would be prone to harm his cattle.
It is to be established from the side, either from this [side] or from that;
he does not make it belong to the homestead;
Paśupati does not become harmful to his cattle.
The gods indeed sent the Gāyatrī to the sacrifice;
she went empty.
Agni gave fiery splendor to her;
that became a goat.
When he gives a goat at the fire-establishment,
he secures fiery splendor.
It is to be given to the Agnīdh;
for the Agnīdh is the mouth of the sacrifice;
by means of the mouth of the sacrifice he brings success to the mouth of the sacrifice.
He gives a milch cow and a draft ox;
thereby he secures all life-span.
All life-span indeed is this: both a milch cow and a draft ox.
These two indeed are the mother and father of the sacrifice: both a milch cow and a draft ox.
Ghee and milk belong to the cow, and the cake and mess of boiled rice belong to the ox.
Regarding that they say:
"He indeed secures a wish-yielding cow who gives a milch cow and a draft ox at the fire-establishment."
Therefore, of whatever cattle he may most desire thriving, of those he should give a two-year-old in age, and an uncastrated two-year-old draft bull.
That is a pair for the thriving, for the procreation of cattle.
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
a two-year-old in age is the cow for purchasing Soma.
When he gives a two-year-old in age,
he secures speech.
He speaks beautifully, who knows thus.
A bolster made of all kinds of thread is to be given.
This bolster made of all kinds of thread is indeed a manifestation of the meters.
When he gives a bolster made of all kinds of thread,
he secures the meters.
Cattle indeed are the meters;
he secures cattle.
Unfit for sacrifice indeed, impure, is he born from wantonness; he sucks human milk.
He gives gold;
by that he purifies himself.
It is of a hundred measures;
a man indeed has a life-span of a hundred, having a hundredfold vigor;
he attains life-span, vigor.
For the former two oblations two [pieces] of thirty measures are to be given,
and for the latter one [a piece] of forty measures.
Thereby he has raised him up;
thereby he becomes progressively more prosperous, more distinguished.
Unborn indeed is a man as long as he does not establish the fire;
he is born only then, when he establishes the fire.
Wearing linen garments, the two [sacrificer and wife] should establish the fire.
Those are to be given to the Adhvaryu.
This linen is indeed a manifestation of the caul;
they strip away the caul.
Having thrown in gold, the fire is to be established.
Gold indeed is the fiery splendor of Agni;
with fiery splendor he establishes it.
It is not to be thrown out;
as if one were to throw out what has been deposited, such is that.
If he were to throw it out, he would be full of longing, hungry.
It is not to be thrown out;
he becomes free from longing, not hungry.

1.6.11.m:
For whomever he desires, "May he be rich in cattle," from the house of one who is very prosperous they should fetch the fire.
Just as cattle were created along with this one being created, so cattle follow him when he is being fetched;
for Agni is Rudra.
Then, for whomever he desires, "May he be an eater of food," from his roasting-pan they should fetch the southern fire.
This one among the fires is the eater of food;
the roasting-pan is the maker of food;
he secures food for him.
Concerning this they say:
"Just as a low-caste man, a native Puklaka, would practice [healing], so is he."
It should only be churned;
it becomes peaceful to the sacrificer, peaceful to the cattle.
He who, not intending to sacrifice with soma, establishes the fire, must not dispense the oblations before a year.
Rudra would be prone to harm his cattle.
These indeed are cattle, namely rice and barley.
Having cooked a four-cup portion of boiled rice of these, he should present it to the Brahmins as if with live grains.
Then, regarding the deities for whom he establishes the fire, if he were not to sacrifice to them, he would be cut off from them;
he would become brooding and hungry.
He must offer ghee to them;
thereby he is not cut off from them;
he becomes unbrooding, not hungry.
In the year he dispenses the oblations;
then Rudra, separated from him by a year, does not harm the cattle.
He has offered the agnihotra for a year;
thereby he found fervor;
he climbed up to the heavenly world together with everything.
Three times indeed did this Virāj stride out: the Gārhapatya, the Āhavanīya, and the Sabhya.
Thereby he attained the Virāj;
the Virāj is food;
food indeed he thus attained.
In the middle of the gaming-ground he should offer for a nobleman with a verse addressed to Varuṇa.
Varuṇa is the king of the gods;
he secures kingship for him.
Having laid down gold, he offers;
he offers only in [a place] possessing fire, possessing a proper seat.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he were to offer in a place without a seat.
He should hand him a hundred dice;
he should sort them out.
A man has a hundred-year lifespan, a hundredfold vigor;
he secures lifespan and vigor.
They should gamble for his cow in the hall on that day;
they must not harm its joints;
he should present it to the members of the assembly.
What he wins with it is to be given to the Brahmins;
thereby he secures food suited for the hall.

1.7.2.m:
The re-establishment [of the fire] is indeed the portion of Agni.
Seeking to obtain this portion, one indeed fails.
If, while establishing [the fire], he should think, "May this [sacrifice] prosper for me," he should, having raised [the fire], establish it again.
The portion that he misses while seeking to obtain it, having reached it, he truly prospers.
Everything is made relating to Agni.
Having given whatever [is made] as a portion to Agni alone, he prospers with all prosperity.
"Materials are not to be gathered;
no yajus is to be performed," they say,
"for he has already gathered materials and performed the yajus."
Regarding this they say:
"The materials are indeed to be gathered;
the yajus is to be performed."
A re-sewn garment is to be given, a renewed chariot, a re-released ox;
these are indeed the forms of the re-establishment;
having attained its forms, he secures it.
Agni, when being raised, rises up toward the waters and the plants.
The darbha grasses are indeed waters and plants.
Because the darbha grasses are upolapas,
having attained him from the waters and the plants, he secures him.
The gods, going to victory against the Asuras, deposited their dear bodies in Agni:
"If they should conquer us, we will flee to these;
and if we should conquer, we will return to these."
Having conquered, the gods sought them:
"All of us together!"
That Agni said:
"Whoever establishes me having me as his deity, he shall unite with these bodies."
The gods established him.
He united with these bodies.
Cattle indeed are the dear bodies of the gods;
they united with cattle.
Therefore, he who, knowing thus, establishes the fire again, unites with these bodies of Agni.
Cattle indeed are the dear bodies of the gods;
he unites with cattle.
Manu, desirous of nourishment, established [it];
he nourished these nourishments;
through that, he prospered.
Tvaṣṭṛ, desirous of cattle, established [it];
these cattle of Tvaṣṭṛ were generated;
through that, he prospered.
Prajāpati, desirous of offspring, established [it];
these offspring of Prajāpati were generated;
through that, he prospered.
He who established him at first, he united with that wealth (vasu);
that is the Punarvasu-nature of Punarvasu.
Therefore [the fire] is to be established under Punarvasu;
for he united again with that wealth.
He who established him prospered;
therefore he is to be established under Anurādhā;
for prosperity;
he truly prospers;
also for pairing.

1.7.4.m:
"Return again with food, again, O Agni, with nourishment and life; / protect us again from distress!" — / he should sacrifice before the fore-offerings.
"Return with wealth, O Agni; swell with a stream, / all-nourishing, from all sides!" — / he should sacrifice after the after-offerings.
With food, with cattle, he, when being raised, rises up together.
[Saying,] "Return again with food," / he secures food and cattle again;
also he makes them portion-holders;
also he prospers the blessings of this sacrifice on both sides.
"Nothing not belonging to Agni is done; why then are the two ghee-portions sacrificed?"
These two ghee-portions are the two eyes of the sacrifice.
If he were to omit the two ghee-portions, he would omit the two eyes of the sacrifice.
He should perform [the verse,] "O Agni, you purify lives" in the place of Soma.
Because it relates to Agni,
thereby it relates to Agni;
because it relates to Pavamāna,
thereby it relates to Soma;
nothing not belonging to Agni is done; they do not omit Soma.
"Agni is the head, the peak of heaven" — he desirous of offspring or desirous of cattle should perform in the place of Soma.
Since it is paired, thereby it possesses procreation;
since it possesses seed, thereby it relates to Soma.
He who raises the fire fails of the sacrifice.
The sacrifice is fivefold.
Because it is on five potsherds,
he grasps the sacrifice again.
It should be on eight potsherds;
for Agni belongs to the Gāyatrī, has the Gāyatrī meter;
relating to Agni indeed is this made, namely the re-establishment;
therefore [it should be] on eight potsherds.
That is not to be heeded.
It should indeed be on five potsherds.
From the fifth season, the sixth season arose;
this is equal: the fifth season and the sixth.
Because it is on five potsherds,
from the year he attains and secures him.
The seasons are procreation;
Agni is the procreator;
because it is on five potsherds,
from procreation the procreator procreates him.

1.8.3.m:
They milk into a cooking-pot.
With this indeed they approach;
for from here it does not spill, nor from there,
for unspiltness.
It is made by an Ārya, with upright potsherd,
for being with the gods,
for it is with the gods.
Belonging to the Asuras indeed is that vessel which is made by a potter, turned on a wheel.
Where it may spill, there he should pour water.
Water indeed is appeasement;
water is expiation;
water is medicine.
Where indeed these [waters] reach of this [earth], there most excellent, most dense plants grow.
If it should break while being milked, having rinsed another cooking-pot, [milk] is to be milked [into it];
for sacrifice is the expiation of sacrifice.
If it should spill when put on [the fire], or when being taken off, or when taken off, or when being scooped up, or when scooped up, or if brought forward for the offering, having poured it down again, another is to be milked into it;
for sacrifice is the expiation of sacrifice.
Having recited a verse to Varuṇa, one should offer with [a verse] to Varuṇa.
Varuṇa indeed seizes of the sacrifice that which suffers injury.
This is an expiation, an atonement.
There should be no pouring upon for one desiring brilliance, desiring Vedic luster,
and for one wishing to overthrow [a rival],
and who should desire, "May a hero be born to me!"
The unpoured cauldron indeed is brilliance, Vedic luster.
He becomes possessed of brilliance, possessed of Vedic luster;
he overthrows whom he wishes to overthrow;
a hero is born to him.
One should offer with ghee for whom there is no pouring upon;
this indeed is unpoured upon.
But there is no expiation for the spilt [milk];
also he does not become, as it were, possessed of cattle.
This milk of cattle is cut off;
he places grief in the cattle;
unappeased brilliance burns the cattle down.
Therefore it should indeed be poured upon.
If he should pour water upon, he would remove the heat.
It is to be poured upon with the water used for washing the cow during milking;
for that is neither water nor milk.
Regarding this they say:
"This indeed spills;
when this is separated from the milking, then it is spilt,
for the trees of this [earth] are concealed [from it]."
So [they say].
It is to be poured upon with water alone;
by a single drop he does not remove the heat;
he appeases the grief of the cattle.
Cattle indeed are brilliance, Vedic luster.
A pairing indeed are water and milk;
from a pairing indeed creatures and cattle are born;
that is a pairing.
From that very pairing the sacrificer is born with offspring and cattle.
One should offer with boiled rice for whom it is to be poured upon;
for this, poured upon, is appeased, fit for sacrifice, a pairing, productive.

1.8.10.m:
Belonging to Rudra while in the cow;
belonging to Vāyu when let loose [to the calf];
belonging to the Aśvins while being milked;
belonging to Soma when milked;
belonging to Varuṇa when set on [the fire];
belonging to Mitra when covered with skin;
belonging to the Maruts when forming drops;
belonging to Pūṣan when boiling up;
belonging to Sarasvatī when overflowing;
belonging to Viṣṇu when brought back;
belonging to heaven when taken off [the fire];
belonging to Tvaṣṭṛ when being drawn up;
belonging to Dhātṛ when drawn up;
belonging to Viśvakarman when raised;
belonging to Savitṛ when being brought forward;
belonging to Heaven and Earth when being borne forth;
belonging to Indra and Agni when set down;
belonging to Prajāpati on the kindling-stick;
belonging to the Viśvedevas when offered.

1.9.1.m:
Thought is the ladle;
thinking is the ghee;
speech is the vedi;
reflection is the sacrificial grass;
intent is Agni;
the known is the agnīdh;
the Lord of Speech is the hotṛ;
the mind is the upavaktṛ;
the out-breath is the oblation;
the sāman is the adhvaryu.
"Go to Indra, svāhā!"
The earth is the hotṛ;
heaven is the adhvaryu;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the agnīdh;
Mitra is the upavaktṛ.
"O Lord of Speech, through the fullest gathered power of speech, to the healthy
master of sacrifice grant desirable things!"
The Lord of Speech drank the soma;
he begat Indra for virile power.
Let soma drink of soma;
let the bright drink of the bright!
Boiled are your somas, O Indra, wind-swelling, hearing our invocation.
Agni is the hotṛ;
the two Aśvins are the two adhvaryus;
Rudra is the agnīdh;
Bṛhaspati is the upavaktṛ.
"O Lord of Speech, Hinvividhe by name,
may we worship your name;
may you worship our name!"
The Lord of Speech drank the soma;
may he place manly courage in us!
Let soma drink of soma;
let the bright drink of the bright!
Boiled are your somas, O Indra, wind-swelling, hearing our invocation.
Mahāhavis is the hotṛ;
Satyahavis is the adhvaryu;
Acittapājas is the agnīdh;
Acittamanas is the upavaktṛ;
Anādhṛṣya and Apratidhṛṣya are the two abhigaras;
Ayāsya is the udgātṛ.
"O Vidhe by name,
may we worship your name;
may you worship our name!
May the lineage of the gods not be cut off, nor that of men!
Homage to Mother Earth!"

1.9.2.m:
Agni with the sacrificial formulas;
Savitṛ with the chants;
Indra with the recitation-delights;
Bṛhaspati with the meters;
Aditi and the waters and the sacrificial grass;
the Ādityas with the ghee offerings;
the Maruts with the sadas and the havirdhāna;
Viṣṇu with consecration and ascetic heat;
Mitra and Varuṇa with the fire-hearths;
the Aśvins with the milk-mixture;
Tvaṣṭṛ with the kindling-stick;
Pūṣan with the utterances of "svāhā".
Speech is the wife of Vāyu;
Pathyā of Pūṣan;
the Earth of Agni;
Senā of Indra;
Dhēnā of Bṛhaspati.
The gāyatrī of the Vasus;
the triṣṭubh of the Rudras;
the jagatī of the Ādityas;
the anuṣṭubh of Mitra;
the virāj of Varuṇa;
the paṅkti of Viṣṇu;
the consecration of Soma.

1.9.3.m:
Prajāpati, indeed, was alone.
He desired:
"Becoming the sacrifice, may I generate creatures."
He arranged himself as the ten-hotṛ sacrifice.
He made thought the ladle, thinking the ghee, speech the vedi, reflection the sacrificial grass.
Having divided himself tenfold and having formed a pair, he sought a support.
He perceived the three-fold vital breaths as the support;
with them he created creatures.
The waxing fifteen-day fortnight broke forth upwards;
with it he created the gods;
those gods swelled upwards.
He who knows thus swells upwards.
The waning fifteen-day fortnight broke forth downwards;
with it he created the asuras;
those asuras collapsed downwards.
His rival collapses who knows thus.
By day he created the gods, by night the asuras;
those gods became white, the asuras black.
By truth he created the gods, by untruth the asuras;
those gods became truth,
the asuras untruth.
With the right hand he created the gods, with the left the asuras;
those gods became vigorous, the asuras powerless.
Then the gods prevailed, the asuras were defeated.
So he who knows thus prevails himself, and his rival is defeated.
He thought: "Where shall I offer?"
Prajāpati found no place where he might offer;
nor did he have anything else to offer from other than breath.
He perceived Indra within his own self as the support;
with "Go to Indra, svāhā!" he breathed out.
Breath is indeed virility; Indra is virility.
Into virility, verily, he offered virility.

1.9.5.m:
Going to heaven, they said:
"From here, give us the sacrificial post!"
"By what support will you find [it] right here?" they said.
They, as the seven-hotṛs, sat down with Aryaman as householder.
Mahāhavis was the hotṛ,
Satyahavis the adhvaryu,
Acittapājas the agnīdh,
Acittamanas the upavaktṛ,
Anādhṛṣya and Apratidhṛṣya the abhigaras,
Ayāsya the udgātṛ.
They drew this cup-offering:
"O Vidhe, by name! May we worship thy name;
mayest thou worship our name!"
They perceived the thirty-three-fold support;
with it they extended this [all].
Indeed, by the seven-hotṛ and by the thirty-three-fold this [world] is connected, since here gods and men bestow upon one another.
The ten-hotṛ is the out-breath;
the four-hotṛ is the eye;
the five-hotṛ is the ear;
the seven-hotṛ is speech and the self.
The ten-hotṛ is the agnihotra;
the four-hotṛ is the new- and full-moon [sacrifices];
the five-hotṛ is the four-month sacrifices;
the seven-hotṛ is the soma rite.
The agnihotra is offered for life;
he reaches a full life who knows thus.
The new- and full-moon [sacrifices] are offered for the eye;
[he is] not [deprived] of his eye at home who knows thus.
The four-month sacrifices are offered for the ear;
[he is] not [deprived] of his ear at home who knows thus.
The soma rite is offered for speech and the self;
[he is] not [deprived] of speech nor of the self at home who knows thus.
Prajāpati, indeed, is the ten-hotṛ;
being one, he has the greatest share of the sacred utterances;
being one, he becomes the greatest who knows thus.
The full-moon [sacrifice] is offered for splendor.
The brahmin belongs to Soma by deity, has the gāyatrī meter;
Agni has the gāyatrī meter;
he has placed him on his own two deities with equal width;
no other rules over his acquisition and preservation.
The new-moon [sacrifice] is offered for life;
for he is brought together, as it were;
he reaches a full life who knows thus.
The soma rite is offered for the heavenly world;
he goes to the heavenly world who knows thus.
He should touch the drawn agnihotra with the ten-hotṛ;
with the ten-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the agnihotra;
he touches it when born, when created.
He should touch the new- and full-moon [sacrifices] with the four-hotṛ;
with the four-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the new- and full-moon [sacrifices];
he touches both when born, when created.
He should touch the four-month sacrifices with the five-hotṛ;
with the five-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the four-month sacrifices;
he touches them when born, when created.
He should touch the soma rite with the seven-hotṛ;
with the seven-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created the soma rite;
he touches it when born, when created.
He should recite the ten-hotṛ before the kindling verses;
Prajāpati, indeed, is the ten-hotṛ;
the kindling verses are the opening of the sacrifice;
he places a pair at the opening of the sacrifice for him.
He should recite the four-hotṛ before the fore-offerings;
with the four-hotṛ, indeed, the gods generated Indra;
he generates Indra in the sacrifice for him;
he sacrifices with a sacrifice accompanied by Indra.
He should recite the five-hotṛ before the oblations;
with the five-hotṛ, indeed, the gods created cattle;
they defeated the asuras;
he creates cattle, he defeats the rival.
He should recite the seven-hotṛ before the after-offerings;
with the seven-hotṛ, indeed, the gods went to heaven;
he goes to heaven.
He should recite the ten-hotṛ before the bahiṣpavamāna;
Prajāpati, indeed, is the ten-hotṛ;
the bahiṣpavamāna is the opening of the sacrifice;
he places a pair at the opening of the sacrifice for him.
He should recite the four-hotṛ before the ājya [chants];
he should recite the five-hotṛ before the midday pavamāna;
he should recite the seven-hotṛ before the ārbhava pavamāna;
with the seven-hotṛ, indeed, the gods went to heaven;
he goes to heaven.
Whoever knows the caturhotṛ-formulas that are to be gratified at each pressing, is gratified with offspring and with cattle.
These four-hotṛs to be gratified at each pressing are the brahmins who know much;
he gratifies them;
they, being gratified, gratify him with offspring and with cattle.
